WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.335
- I'm a big fan of the WNBA.

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:06.089
- You think about the NBA, in its 25th year, was not

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:08.758
where it is now in year 75.
- Right.

00:00:08.758 --> 00:00:12.220
- And so it's so much opportunity and so much growth

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.179
that's coming.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:16.474
But what I really love about the W is the activism.

00:00:16.474 --> 00:00:20.603
I just love the way that the W stands together.
- Mmhmm.
- You know? Locked arms.

00:00:20.603 --> 00:00:22.605
It's not one person. It's the whole league.

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:25.400
And every time something social,

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.943
something that hits home,

00:00:26.943 --> 00:00:29.320
something that's important to talk about,

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.907
that we need voices, it's the W who steps up first.

00:00:32.907 --> 00:00:34.868
- Yeah, that's one of the things I love most about it

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:37.871
is like, I feel like everyone in there is so empowered

00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:40.081
to just, like, say whatever they think.

00:00:40.081 --> 00:00:42.459
And I think it's like the demographic that we have too.

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:46.379
It's like predominantly, like, gay Black women in the league.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.423
- Mmhmm.
- And so that's like a minority.

00:00:48.423 --> 00:00:50.759
And so we feel like now we have the platform

00:00:50.759 --> 00:00:52.969
where we can talk about these things,

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:54.095
and then we see other things,

00:00:54.095 --> 00:00:55.472
like other injustices happening.

00:00:55.472 --> 00:00:57.599
And, you know, maybe we knew how that felt,

00:00:57.599 --> 00:00:58.767
so we wanna speak out against that.

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:01.186
Or, I just feel like they're so fearless.

00:01:01.186 --> 00:01:03.146
And, like, as a group, we're so powerful—
- Facts.

00:01:03.146 --> 00:01:05.815
- That we know, like, the platform that we have now.

00:01:05.815 --> 00:01:07.859
And so whenever we see something that we don't think

00:01:07.859 --> 00:01:10.945
is right, we feel like not only should we do it,

00:01:10.945 --> 00:01:15.283
but it's kind of, like, on our shoulders to be able to speak out.

00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:17.160
- I think one of the things about the WNBA

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.411
and their social activism,

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:20.080
it was never a one-and-done for them.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.415
They've been doing it for well over a decade,

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:24.709
probably for the full 25 years.

00:01:24.709 --> 00:01:28.088
I'm Cathy Engelbert, the commissioner of the WNBA.

00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:31.758
I think the events of 2020, where we had the entire league

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:35.804
in one place in the WNBA bubble in Florida at IMG Academy,

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:39.516
and I think what happened then is now millions know that

00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:42.769
the WNBA players have such strong social justice stances.

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:44.771
And I think it's really important that people know

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:45.688
what they stand for.

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:48.066
And they've been such leaders in this area.

00:01:48.066 --> 00:01:50.693
- As the NBA, we look to the W first—
- Mmhmm.

00:01:50.693 --> 00:01:53.029
- And see what they're doing more times than not.

00:01:53.029 --> 00:01:57.117
They speak on, you know, things that are going on in the world

00:01:57.117 --> 00:01:57.992
before we do.

00:01:57.992 --> 00:01:58.827
You know what I'm saying?

00:01:58.827 --> 00:02:00.662
I think we kinda follow their lead to a point.

00:02:00.662 --> 00:02:02.872
So, I think that's the best part about the W is how

00:02:02.872 --> 00:02:04.040
they're so ahead of everything.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.167
And just to support that—you know,

00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:07.502
obviously they give us, and we give back.

00:02:07.502 --> 00:02:10.630
- I feel like it can even get better.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.550
Like, the relationship between the W and the NBA.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.010
Everybody come together and voice their opinion

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.680
about things that's going on in the world.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:22.350
- I feel like la—um, not last year, 2020. That was like—
- Yeah.
- We kinda wanna stand

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.435
in solidarity to you guys when you stepped out of the game.
- Mmhmm, right.

00:02:24.435 --> 00:02:25.603
- Like, we want that too.

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:27.981
We wanna be, like, with you guys—
- Right.
- And making sure that

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:29.732
we're standing as, like, a united front.

00:02:29.732 --> 00:02:30.608
- For sure.

00:02:30.608 --> 00:02:33.570
- Coming off that playoffs—
- Mmhmm.
- It was incredible.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.363
If you love basketball, to be able to watch that

00:02:35.363 --> 00:02:37.740
playoffs, the WNBA playoffs, was amazing.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.117
How can you guys assist?

00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:42.203
Or how can we all help the popularity continue to grow?

00:02:42.203 --> 00:02:45.039
- Us as the NBA, guys going to their games,

00:02:45.039 --> 00:02:46.958
showing love and support.
- Mmhmm.

00:02:46.958 --> 00:02:48.251
- I see that a lot more.

00:02:48.251 --> 00:02:51.421
- We just awarded the inaugural Kobe

00:02:51.421 --> 00:02:55.341
and Gigi Bryant WNBA Advocacy Award at the NBA All-Star Game

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:57.969
this year to Chris Paul, who's been a huge advocate.

00:02:57.969 --> 00:02:59.971
Not only for youth sports,

00:02:59.971 --> 00:03:02.348
but also of the WNBA, their players.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:05.268
It's those kind of advocates, like Kobe Bryant was

00:03:05.268 --> 00:03:08.354
for the women's game, that we need to expand upon.

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:10.481
And I think NBA players have done that.

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:12.775
I've never seen such great support.

00:03:12.775 --> 00:03:15.361
There's a reason why the WNBA has been

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:18.198
the only women's professional sport to last 25 years.

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:21.117
And that's double any other women's professional league

00:03:21.117 --> 00:03:23.161
in America. Think about that.

00:03:23.161 --> 00:03:25.163
It's because we share the NBA brand.

00:03:25.163 --> 00:03:27.290
- I just bought my first WNBA jersey the other day.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:31.085
- [Collier:] Nice.
- So, you know, wear that in the tunnel, you know, as the season gets closer.

00:03:31.085 --> 00:03:33.755
I like the, you know, kind of the orange hoodie thing they did

00:03:33.755 --> 00:03:34.589
a couple years ago.

00:03:34.589 --> 00:03:36.966
I thought that was, like, super cool to kinda build momentum

00:03:36.966 --> 00:03:38.509
towards your guys' season.

00:03:38.509 --> 00:03:40.386
But I think at the same time, like we were talking

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:43.681
about earlier, you were talking about, like, controversy helps too.

00:03:43.681 --> 00:03:44.515
You know what I'm saying?
- [Collier:] Mmhmm.

00:03:44.515 --> 00:03:46.935
- Like, the more conversations we have about the league,

00:03:46.935 --> 00:03:48.019
like, good or bad—
- Yeah, yeah.

00:03:48.019 --> 00:03:51.064
- Like, it does help, and it brings attention.

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:53.483
And there's so much great basketball played

00:03:53.483 --> 00:03:56.736
that, you know, the more people that actually sit down and watch a game,

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:57.737
they'll fall in love with it.

00:03:57.737 --> 00:04:01.449
- You guys just got a $75 million, you know, capital raise.

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:03.034
- [Collier:] Yep.
- Can you tell us about it?

00:04:04.035 --> 00:04:04.994
What does that—where do that money go?

00:04:04.994 --> 00:04:07.121
How does that help the league grow?

00:04:07.121 --> 00:04:09.666
- I mean, I honestly don't know where the money—like,
I don't know how it's distributed.

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:12.085
- The $75 million capital raise was really

00:04:12.085 --> 00:04:14.712
a watershed moment for women's professional sports.

00:04:14.712 --> 00:04:18.424
And I think once again, the WNBA, we're leading around

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:20.843
how to deploy that capital to grow the game.

00:04:20.843 --> 00:04:21.928
Both the youth side of the game,

00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:24.097
as well as the professional side of the game

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:25.265
and everything in between.

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:27.225
And so we're gonna make it easier to be a fan.

00:04:27.225 --> 00:04:31.396
We're gonna, you know, look at a five-year business transformation

00:04:31.396 --> 00:04:33.439
to, you know, change our digital products,

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:35.358
meet our fans where they are.

00:04:35.358 --> 00:04:37.902
They're very digital. They want content.

00:04:37.902 --> 00:04:40.071
They want year-round content.

00:04:40.071 --> 00:04:41.572
We're also looking at globalizing the game.

00:04:41.572 --> 00:04:45.994
We're looking at innovation like NFTs and sports betting.

00:04:45.994 --> 00:04:49.455
And ESPN just launched the first-ever WNBA fantasy game,

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:50.290
for instance.

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:52.000
So, how do you draw in that fan,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:52.875
keep that fan,

00:04:52.875 --> 00:04:53.710
get them to come to arenas,

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:57.547
get them to watch on our very different platforms?

00:04:57.547 --> 00:05:00.174
And we're just continuing looking at optical tracking

00:05:00.174 --> 00:05:02.635
and wearables and lots of different innovative things,

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:04.178
which, before raising this capital,

00:05:04.178 --> 00:05:05.847
we didn't have the ability to do.

00:05:05.847 --> 00:05:07.765
- You have to spend money to make money.

00:05:07.765 --> 00:05:09.225
- Right.
- And that's always people's arguments.

00:05:09.225 --> 00:05:11.811
Like, if you want people to come, you have to promote revenue.

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:14.063
Like, you have to put in money to something for it to grow.

00:05:14.063 --> 00:05:16.983
- Does that make you excited about where the future

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:19.360
of the game is going and, you know, and you can see, like,

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.530
"OK, now we have people believing in us a little bit more"?

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:23.489
- Yeah, it's really exciting.

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:24.741
And we have some great partners.

00:05:24.741 --> 00:05:27.285
Like, we—they call each other Changemakers.
- Mmhmm.

00:05:27.285 --> 00:05:28.745
- Cuz they're, like, trying to make a difference

00:05:28.745 --> 00:05:30.788
in women's sports and in our league.

00:05:30.788 --> 00:05:32.790
So, it's awesome to see that because, like, you guys

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:34.625
were talking, it was funny about the stylist.

00:05:34.625 --> 00:05:36.252
You're like, "Do you care what the price tag is?"

00:05:36.252 --> 00:05:37.920
And you're like, "Nah, I got that new contract.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:38.963
I don't care."
- [Laughing]

00:05:38.963 --> 00:05:40.923
- I'm like—I'm still, like, budgeting on my contract.
- [Laughing]

00:05:42.425 --> 00:05:43.551
- So, it's awesome.

00:05:43.551 --> 00:05:44.552
Like I said, our new CA was really good.

00:05:44.552 --> 00:05:46.596
Like, the money has gone up.
- Yeah.
- And it's better than

00:05:46.596 --> 00:05:49.182
what it used to be, but it's, like, nowhere near what we feel like

00:05:49.182 --> 00:05:51.100
we deserve or where we want it to be.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.895
- The steps the WNBA is making around pay

00:05:53.895 --> 00:05:55.438
is very multidimensional.

00:05:55.438 --> 00:05:57.440
So the owners and the league stepped up

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:58.858
in the last collective bargaining agreement

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:00.443
to triple the pay of the top players.

00:06:00.443 --> 00:06:04.489
To give a lot of other offseason capabilities

00:06:04.489 --> 00:06:08.076
in order to grow not only their on-court skills,

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:09.744
but their off-the-court skills

00:06:09.744 --> 00:06:11.913
The average tenure of a WNBA player,

00:06:11.913 --> 00:06:13.164
any professional athlete, is not that long.

00:06:13.164 --> 00:06:17.085
And they need to, you know, be gaining skills

00:06:17.085 --> 00:06:19.003
while they're still playing in order to see

00:06:19.003 --> 00:06:20.880
what they're gonna do post their playing careers.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.590
So, we've got internships.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:24.592
We've opened up coaching opportunities,

00:06:24.592 --> 00:06:28.179
not only in the WNBA but the NBA and the G League.
And, you know, there's some—

00:06:29.806 --> 00:06:32.350
Kristi Toliver's a great example of that, you know, coaching

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.144
for Dallas this season, in the offseason.

00:06:35.144 --> 00:06:37.522
We also have league marketing opportunities

00:06:37.522 --> 00:06:39.023
and team marketing opportunities.

00:06:39.023 --> 00:06:40.942
So, players and what we're doing over the course

00:06:40.942 --> 00:06:43.653
of this collective bargaining cycle is giving players

00:06:43.653 --> 00:06:46.155
a lot more opportunities to earn more money.

00:06:46.155 --> 00:06:48.783
We introduced a new special competition during

00:06:48.783 --> 00:06:49.700
the 2021 season.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:52.662
This year will be the second, called the Commissioner's Cup,

00:06:52.662 --> 00:06:56.374
where just the winner of that will get $30,000, each player.

00:06:56.374 --> 00:06:59.877
So, more opportunities for players to make more money

00:06:59.877 --> 00:07:04.424
in order to, you know, have our players really prioritize the WNBA

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:06.134
as their season and their job

00:07:07.051 --> 00:07:09.429
- In the NBA, we talked about how social media

00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:12.765
versus mainstream media—
- Mmhmm.
- How does it help or affect?

00:07:12.765 --> 00:07:15.226
I feel like in the WNBA, you guys haven't had

00:07:15.226 --> 00:07:17.395
a lot of mainstream media, but the social component

00:07:17.395 --> 00:07:19.981
has been, has been pretty big.
- [Collier:] Mmhmm.

00:07:19.981 --> 00:07:21.607
- Right? Whether it's activism,

00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:23.568
whether it's the walk through the tunnels.

00:07:23.568 --> 00:07:26.529
I love watching the women when—
- Yeah.
- They come in with they swag.

00:07:27.947 --> 00:07:31.159
How do you think the social component of the world

00:07:31.159 --> 00:07:32.785
has really helped the WNBA?

00:07:32.785 --> 00:07:34.036
- It's helped us a lot, especially

00:07:34.036 --> 00:07:37.248
because, like, one of the biggest problems is we're not on TV, ya know?

00:07:37.248 --> 00:07:39.834
- Yeah. Mmhmm.
- When people watch us, they become fans.

00:07:39.834 --> 00:07:42.170
Like, it's good basketball.
- Right.
- It's just, it's not accessible.

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:44.839
No one wants to go on Twitter or Facebook to, like, search

00:07:44.839 --> 00:07:45.673
for a game.
- Right.

00:07:45.673 --> 00:07:47.508
- We only get true fans doing that.

00:07:47.508 --> 00:07:51.637
So, if there was, like, a Game 5 for the WNBA championship,

00:07:51.637 --> 00:07:54.807
it wasn't gonna be on ESPN 1.  Like, that's crazy. So, we have to—

00:07:56.767 --> 00:07:58.561
like, you guys supporting us is a huge part of it,

00:07:58.561 --> 00:07:59.395
and it's awesome.

00:07:59.395 --> 00:08:02.982
We also need the stuff—like, we have to be able to be on TV.

00:08:02.982 --> 00:08:04.609
- I wanna ask a question.
- Yeah.

00:08:06.068 --> 00:08:08.988
- Like, people always talk about, like, that they think

00:08:08.988 --> 00:08:10.740
the W should have more teams.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:12.950
I played in Sacramento, and, like, the Monarchs,

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:14.619
like, they got it all hung up, right?

00:08:14.619 --> 00:08:16.537
And, like, they always talk about the legends of the Monarchs,

00:08:16.537 --> 00:08:19.332
how good they were and things like that.

00:08:19.332 --> 00:08:21.083
Like, but also—and I also see, like, the other side of the argument.

00:08:21.083 --> 00:08:22.710
Like, if there is more teams and that's, like, more money

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:25.630
that's gotta go and then what happens to the salaries

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:26.464
and things like that.

00:08:26.464 --> 00:08:28.216
So what's your take on that?

00:08:28.216 --> 00:08:29.717
- We definitely need more teams.

00:08:29.717 --> 00:08:33.721
Like, there's just too much talent to accommodate 144 people.
- Yeah.

00:08:33.721 --> 00:08:35.515
- You have really good people not making rosters,

00:08:35.515 --> 00:08:37.266
and the people who are on the rosters,

00:08:37.266 --> 00:08:38.184
like, you're not getting playing time

00:08:38.184 --> 00:08:40.019
because the talent is, like, so concentrated.

00:08:40.019 --> 00:08:42.146
You have, like, rookies who aren't playing for years

00:08:42.146 --> 00:08:43.814
because they're just behind good players.

00:08:43.814 --> 00:08:46.943
- We're being very thoughtful about expansion.

00:08:46.943 --> 00:08:48.653
I think it is on the horizon.

00:08:48.653 --> 00:08:51.614
But we need to have our finances in a better place

00:08:51.614 --> 00:08:52.448
and the economic model

00:08:52.448 --> 00:08:55.743
so that new owners who start up a team in a new city

00:08:55.743 --> 00:08:57.078
can thrive when they come in.

00:08:57.078 --> 00:09:00.039
When you do only have 12 teams, 144 players,

00:09:00.039 --> 00:09:02.667
there's a lot of players who get drafted in the second

00:09:02.667 --> 00:09:05.753
and third round of our WNBA draft who may not make a team.

00:09:05.753 --> 00:09:08.881
And Napheesa's right about thinking about

00:09:08.881 --> 00:09:11.551
why there's so much talent out there that doesn't make

00:09:11.551 --> 00:09:12.385
a WNBA team.

00:09:12.385 --> 00:09:14.679
And we've gotta expand those opportunities,

00:09:14.679 --> 00:09:17.014
which we're in the process of thinking through

00:09:17.014 --> 00:09:19.100
- When the WNBA is in year 50

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.394
and they're celebrating their 50th year,

00:09:21.394 --> 00:09:22.979
the NBA's celebrating its 100th year.

00:09:22.979 --> 00:09:25.940
But what do you want the WNBA to be? What's the vision?

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:28.651
What's the goal in 25 years?

00:09:28.651 --> 00:09:29.860
- Pheesa's seeing million-dollar contracts.
- I wanna see million-dollar contracts!

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:32.154
- Yeah, facts.
- There you go, million-dollar contracts.
- For sure.

00:09:32.154 --> 00:09:36.909
- That's a fact. And I wanna get on Twitter and see people debating about, you know,

00:09:36.909 --> 00:09:39.787
whoever's next up. Like, they better than Diana?

00:09:39.787 --> 00:09:40.705
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:09:40.705 --> 00:09:43.207
- Are they better than Sue?
- [Laughing]

00:09:43.207 --> 00:09:44.208
- You know what I'm saying?
- Who's gonna be the Michael Jordan,

00:09:44.208 --> 00:09:45.042
where they're always competing?

00:09:45.042 --> 00:09:46.127
- Yeah, exactly. That's what I wanna see.

00:09:46.127 --> 00:09:47.503
- Yeah.
- You know what I'm saying?

00:09:47.503 --> 00:09:50.506
- So what I'd like the WNBA to look like in 25 years

00:09:50.506 --> 00:09:54.093
at our 50th anniversary is it really needs to be thriving

00:09:54.093 --> 00:09:57.054
as a really legitimized sports,

00:09:57.054 --> 00:09:58.514
media and entertainment property.

00:09:58.514 --> 00:10:00.933
Where the players are household names.

00:10:00.933 --> 00:10:04.478
Where fans are consuming our content in whatever way

00:10:04.478 --> 00:10:08.190
in 25 years consumers are consuming content.

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:10.610
Whether that's around AI,

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:14.572
augmented reality, VR, you know, all of the technologies

00:10:14.572 --> 00:10:16.449
that are emerging today.

00:10:16.449 --> 00:10:18.117
And that we're a global sport.

00:10:18.117 --> 00:10:19.869
The future of the game is bright.

00:10:19.869 --> 00:10:23.581
You see the popularity of the sport at the NCAA level.

00:10:23.581 --> 00:10:25.082
You know, girls between eight and 18,

00:10:25.082 --> 00:10:27.501
there's five to six million girls playing basketball

00:10:27.501 --> 00:10:29.003
in America today.

00:10:29.003 --> 00:10:30.421
So, I mean, it's a really positive sign

00:10:30.421 --> 00:10:31.964
for the future of the sport.

00:10:31.964 --> 00:10:35.092
And I think we will be able to go from survive to thrive

00:10:35.092 --> 00:10:36.802
over the next 25 years.

00:10:36.802 --> 00:10:38.387
- I feel like talent that's coming in, it's just,

00:10:38.387 --> 00:10:40.264
it's just basketball

00:10:40.264 --> 00:10:42.099
just keep getting better over and over.

00:10:42.099 --> 00:10:44.644
- Yeah.
- Year after year.
- We keep talking about, like, the more accessible

00:10:44.644 --> 00:10:46.354
the game is on TV—

00:10:46.354 --> 00:10:48.147
and obviously they're doing things on social media—

00:10:48.147 --> 00:10:49.273
but the more accessible the game is—
- Yeah.

00:10:49.273 --> 00:10:51.150
- When people watch, they become fans.
- Mmhmm.

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:52.735
- Like, no doubt about that.

00:10:52.735 --> 00:10:55.279
[Upbeat music]

